## Authentication

The provider authenticates with Administration API client credentials. To create them:

1. In Camunda Hub, click **Organization > Manage organization**.
1. In the **Administration API** tab, click **Create new credentials**.
1. Grant the scopes for the resources you manage:

   | Resource | Scope |
   |---|---|
   | `camunda_cluster` and the `camunda_channel`, `camunda_region` and `camunda_cluster_plan_type` data sources | Cluster |
   | `camunda_cluster_client` | Zeebe Client |
   | `camunda_cluster_connector_secret` | Connector Secrets |
   | `camunda_cluster_ip_whitelist` | IP allowlist |
   | `camunda_organization_member` | Members |

1. Copy the client ID and client secret. The secret is shown only once.

Pass the credentials through environment variables, so they stay out of your configuration:

```shell
export CAMUNDA_CONSOLE_CLIENT_ID="<client-id>"
export CAMUNDA_CONSOLE_CLIENT_SECRET="<client-secret>"
```

These are the variable names Camunda Hub shows when it creates the credentials. Values set in the
provider block take precedence over the environment.

| Argument | Environment variable | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `client_id` | `CAMUNDA_CONSOLE_CLIENT_ID` | — |
| `client_secret` | `CAMUNDA_CONSOLE_CLIENT_SECRET` | — |
| `api_url` | `CAMUNDA_CONSOLE_BASE_URL` | `https://api.cloud.camunda.io` |
| `token_url` | `CAMUNDA_OAUTH_URL` | `https://login.cloud.camunda.io/oauth/token` |
| `audience` | `CAMUNDA_CONSOLE_OAUTH_AUDIENCE` | the host of `api_url` |

## Protecting clusters

The Administration API can't change a cluster's `plan_type`, `generation` or `auto_update` in place,
so changing them replaces the cluster, which deletes it and all its data. Add
`lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }` to clusters you can't lose, as in the example above,
so such a plan fails instead.

